From 5b4acf8c73d343a5eced0bec0bcfc3e78ca6bcd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MiaAI-Lab <mia.ailab1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 15:56:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 57/63] glm cuda: paired prompt steps beside DFlash2, each
 chunk tapped as it would be alone

TF_GLM_PROMPT_PAIR refused every step while a drafter was loaded, so with --drafter (the
recipe's DFlash2) a prompt never paired and its exchanges stayed exposed. A pair now carries
the drafter's taps:

- The twin (Pair.init's new `drafter`) holds the second chunk's tap rows, a chunk's worth of
  DFlash2 rows; the first chunk keeps taps_prefill.
- Each chunk taps only when it reaches into the drafter's last window (prefillChunk's rule,
  dflash.promptRun).
- pairBody taps each ffn piece on the side stream right after its glue there posts the rows
  (tapPiece: #1623's split rule, the rank's own rows; in-process ranks copy the owners' rows
  in first), and once both chunks' last sites are in, shares each chunk's taps on the main
  stream (#1623's shareTaps) while the side stream holds nothing else.
- The drafter then ingests a's rows from taps_prefill and b's from the twin's
  (Drafter.promptFrom), in that order, as two chunks one at a time do; the MTP head's prompt
  rows stay skipped beside a drafter.

The pass's kernels, their inputs and their order per chunk are pairing's own, and the taps
are the same kernel on the same rows, so the drafter's context is the one an unpaired step
builds.

